UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 12, Lesson 1: Shadows, reflection and mirrors', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Shadows form when light is blocked; reflection happens when light bounces off surfaces — mirrors are shiny reflectors that form clear images.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nExplain the difference between a shadow on the wall and your face in a mirror.\n\nFEEDBACK\nShadow is a dark shape from blocked light; mirror image is light reflected to your eyes from the shiny surface.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDemonstrate and photograph/draw one shadow and one mirror reflection. Label light source, object and where light is blocked or bounced.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Shadows form when light is blocked; reflection happens when light bounces off surfaces — mirrors are shiny reflectors that form clear images.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Demonstrate and photograph/draw one shadow and one mirror reflection. Label light source, object and where light is blocked or bounced. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about shadow linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine.. Correction: Shadow is a dark shape from blocked light; with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses shadow. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about shadow, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about shadow. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for shadow. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for shadow. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Shadow is a dark shape from blocked light"],"question":"Explain the difference between a shadow on the wall and your face in a mirror. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for shadow; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine","Shadows form when light is blocked","Shadow is a dark shape from blocked light","Careful use of reflection and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with shadow?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named reflection and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name reflection and explain why it matters only, without linking it to shadow","Apply shadow to new material and name the reflection and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine"],"question":"Which response best applies shadow with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone"],"question":"New examples: steam from a kettle, a germinating bean, a plastic dinosaur, a granite pebble. Which claim about shadow is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A label copied from the title with no evidence","Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine","A named observation plus how it supports the claim","A longer story with no observation"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about shadow?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about shadow with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Shadow is a dark shape from blocked light","Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer"],"question":"Using this science idea — Shadows form when light is blocked; — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about shadow?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about shadow should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true"],"question":"Which prediction about shadow can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about shadow. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses shadow. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about shadow linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for shadow. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about shadow, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine.. Correction: Shadow is a dark shape from blocked light; with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w12-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise shadow.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Physical World","success_criteria":["I can use shadow with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify reflection and explain why it matters.","I can practise mirror independently on a new prompt.","I can notice bounce needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Shadows form when light is blocked; reflection happens when light bounces off surfaces — mirrors are shiny reflectors that form clear images.","options":["Shadows form when light is blocked","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about shadow with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOpaque book → shadow. Mirror → you see a reflected image. Dull black paper reflects poorly compared with a mirror.\n\nTRY FIRST\nExplain the difference between a shadow on the wall and your face in a mirror.\n\nFEEDBACK\nShadow is a dark shape from blocked light; mirror image is light reflected to your eyes from the shiny surface.","explain":"Shadows form when light is blocked; reflection happens when light bounces off surfaces — mirrors are shiny reflectors that form clear images.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Shadows vs reflection.","caption":"Shiny ≠ always perfect mirror.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shadow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">blocked light</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Mirror</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">bounced light</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Opaque book → shadow. Mirror → you see a reflected image. Dull black paper reflects poorly compared with a mirror. Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Opaque book → shadow. Mirror → you see a reflected image. Dull black paper reflects poorly compared with a mirror. Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about shadow with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOpaque book → shadow. Mirror → you see a reflected image. Dull black paper reflects poorly compared with a mirror. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Clear bounce vs scatter.","caption":"Two demos labelled.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Label</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">source object path</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">all shine = mirror image</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about shadow with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine. Here is a more productive model: Opaque book → shadow. Mirror → you see a reflected image. Dull black paper reflects poorly compared with a mirror.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nShadow is a dark shape from blocked light; mirror image is light reflected to your eyes from the shiny surface.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Calling every shiny thing a perfect mirror ignores scattered reflection on rough shine. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about shadow with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nShadows form when light is blocked; reflection happens when light bounces off surfaces — mirrors are shiny reflectors that form clear images. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 12, Lesson 1: Shadows, reflection and mirrors","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Light and sound","week":12,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nOpaque book → shadow. Mirror → you see a reflected image. Dull black paper reflects poorly compared with a mirror.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Shadows vs reflection.","caption":"Shiny ≠ always perfect mirror.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=